➡️ The server understands data ONLY by name, not by id.
    <form>
    <input type="text" id="username">
    <input type="submit">
    </form>

    What happens?
        You type something
        Click Submit
        ❌ Nothing is sent to server
        Because → no name attribute

With name ✔️
<form>
    <input type="text" id="username" name="username">
    <input type="submit">
</form>

✔️ Server receives the value correctly

| Attribute | Used for                            |
| --------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `id`      | Label linking, CSS, JavaScript      |
| `name`    | Sending data to server              |
| Unique?   | `id` must be unique                 |
| Reusable? | `name` can repeat (radio, checkbox) |

-> Why SAME name in Radio Buttons?
<input type="radio" name="gender" value="Male">
<input type="radio" name="gender" value="Female">

Why?
Same name → browser allows only one selection
Data sent:
gender=Male

-> EXAM ANSWER (2–3 lines)

The name attribute is used to identify form data when the form is submitted.
Data is sent to the server as name–value pairs.
Without the name attribute, input data is not submitted.

Final Rule (Remember this)

❗ No name → No data submission
